<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xml:id="sec-rxns2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Ionic equations and Net Ionic equations</title>

	<p>
		When the reactants in a reaction are aqueous, the standard way of writing a
		reaction works, but depending on what we’re trying to show, we can sometimes
		do better.
		For instance, writing <m>NaCl\ \text{(aq)}</m> in a reaction tells us that sodium chloride is
		dissolved in water.
		It doesn’t show us what has happened to the sodium chloride through.
		When ionic compounds dissolve in water, they dissociate into their ions, with
		both the cations and anions forming bonds with the dipole of water.
		A better way of depicting this in a chemical reaction would be to instead
		write <m>NaCl\ \text{(aq)}</m> as <m>Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + Cl^-\ \text{(aq)}</m>.
		This is what we do when we write an
		<term><xref ref="def-ionicequation" text="custom">ionic equation</xref></term>;
		all of the reactants and products that are aqueous are instead written as
		their ions that are dissolved in water.
		Two important notes:
	</p>

	<p>
		1.
		If there is more than one ion, then this is specified with a coefficient in
		front of the ion...NOT with a subscript after it.
		2.
		Polyatomic ions will not break apart
	</p>

	<p>
		Both of these rules are illustrated when we write <m>Na_2SO_4\ \text{(aq)}</m> as
		<m>2Na^+ + SO_4^{2-}</m>.
	</p>

	<p>
		A
		<term><xref ref="def-netionicequation" text="custom">net ionic equation</xref></term>
		is an ionic equation that only lists the ions that actually react in the
		reaction.
		In order to write a net ionic equation we begin with an ionic equation and
		then delete any ions that are aqueous in both the reactants and products.
		These ions are called spectator ions because they don’t participate in the
		reaction themselves.
		See the example below:
	</p>

	<p>
		Standard reaction equation:
		</p>
<me>
			Na_3PO_4\ \text{(aq)} + FeCl_3\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow FePO_4\ \text{(s)} + 3NaCl\ \text{(aq)}
		</me>

	<p>
		Ionic equation:
		</p>
<me>
			3Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + PO_4^{3-}\ \text{(aq)} + Fe^{3+}\ \text{(aq)} + 3Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow FePO_4\ \text{(s)} +
			3Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + 3Cl^-\ \text{(aq)}
		</me>

	<p>
		Net ionic equation:
		</p>
<me>
			Fe^{3+}\ \text{(aq)} + PO_4^{3-}\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow FePO_4\ \text{(s)}
		</me>

	<exercises>
		<title>Exercise 3</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Write ionic and net ionic equations for the following reactions
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>Na_3PO_4\ \text{(aq)} + FeCl_3\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow FePO_4\ \text{(s)} + 3NaCl\ \text{(aq)}</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Ionic equation:
						<m>
							3Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + PO_4^{3-}\ \text{(aq)} + Fe^{3+}\ \text{(aq)} + 3Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow
							FePO_4\ \text{(s)} + 3Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + 3Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} </m>
					</p>

					<p>
						Net ionic:
						<m> PO_4^{3-}\ \text{(aq)} + Fe^{3+}\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow FePO_4\ \text{(s)}
						</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>K_2CO_3\ \text{(aq)} + ZnBr_2\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow ZnCO_3\ \text{(s)} + 2KBr\ \text{(aq)}</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Ionic equation:
						<m>2K^+\ \text{(aq)} + CO_3^{2-}\ \text{(aq)} + Zn^{2+}\ \text{(aq)} + 2Br^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow
								ZnCO_3\ \text{(s)} + 2K^+\ \text{(aq)} + 2Br^-\ \text{(aq)}
							</m>
					</p>

					<p>
						Net ionic:
						<m>
								CO_3^{2-}\ \text{(aq)} + Zn^{2+}\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow
								ZnCO_3\ \text{(s)}
							</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>2Cs\ \text{(s)} + FeCl_2\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow 2CsCl\ \text{(aq)} + Fe\ \text{(s)}</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
							Ionic equation:
							<m>
								2Cs\ \text{(s)} + Fe^{2+}\ \text{(aq)} + 2Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow 2Cs^+\ \text{(aq)} + 2Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} + Fe\ \text{(s)}
							</m>
						</p><p>
							Net ionic: <m>2Cs\ \text{(s)} + Fe^{2+}\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow 2Cs^+\ \text{(aq)} + Fe\ \text{(s)}</m>
						</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>F_2\ \text{(g)} + 2NaCl\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow Cl_2\ \text{(g)} + 2NaF\ \text{(aq)}</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
							Ionic equation: <m>F_2\ \text{(g)} + 2Na^+\ \text{(aq)} + 2Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow Cl_2\ \text{(g)} + 2Na^+\ \text{(aq)} +
								2F^-\ \text{(aq)}
							</m>
						</p><p>
							Net ionic: <m>F_2\ \text{(g)} + 2Cl^-\ \text{(aq)} \rightarrow Cl_2\ \text{(g)} + 2F^-\ \text{(aq)}</m>
						</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
	</exercises>
</section>
